  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DEC Ukraine Humanitarian Appeal DPI Phase 2b</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Context: The scale of need in Ukraine has grown: OCHA suggest 16 million people need urgent assistance but estimate only two-thirds are receiving it. Alongside provision of emergency material assistance, there is a need to respond to other emerging issues such as trauma, accommodation and welfare entitlements. There is also a key need for winterisation support, as temperatures often reach -20C. Prior to war, dozens of people sleeping rough would freeze to death from hypothermia every year. The war has caused a sharp rise in homelessness (in its broad definition) so there is a risk this will be multiplied if support is not put in place. 

Programme overview: Depaul&apos;s project builds on the foundations and outcomes achieved during DEC Phases 1 and 2a. The programme will sustain provision of emergency assistance and augment this with winterisation support, whilst also developing holistic support and accommodation services to meet the wider needs of affected populations.  Winterisation related activities were predominantly delivered in phase 2a but will continue into phase 2b as needed. 

In order to better track and analyse the measures to strengthen accountability to affected populations, five new outputs and indicators have been added to this phase. These will track the number of feedback/complaints received through project mechanisms and their successful resolution, as well as community satisfaction with project services and awareness of the feedback mechanisms through post-distribution monitoring.  

Key activities include:
Outcome A &#x2013; affected populations have access to food assistance
[Outcome B &#x2013; Hygiene - Discontinued]
Outcome C &#x2013; Affected populations are supported with winterisation
Outcome D &#x2013; Affected populations have access to holistic support services (legal, MHPSS)
Outcome E &#x2013; Affected population have access to accommodation
Outcome F - Affected populations access safe and dignified feedback mechanisms</narrative>
  </description>
